Morning,

I need help with something that sounds simple but probably is not.

Product needs to define successful channel adoption as sustained useful activ=
ity rather than enrolment or one event. The discussion is currently being dri=
ven by anecdotes, and the teams involved are describing the same customers di=
fferently.

The questions I would ask in the room are:
1. What would we expect to see if customers return to assisted channels after=
 failure?
2. What evidence would instead support that first successful use predicts con=
tinued use?
3. How do we rule out that adoption differs by product and customer need?

This has returned because the previous answer was useful but not strong enoug=
h to become a standing rule. Use August 2020 as the new evidence point rather=
 than recycling the earlier conclusion. Please work towards which onboarding =
journey and success metric should change. A useful output would be a model-re=
ady table, data dictionary and an honest baseline.

Use monthly account snapshots, status events, limits, product enrolments and =
signatories; transaction-level timestamps, channels, amounts, statuses and er=
ror context; customer contacts, complaints and suggestions; ATM attempts, hos=
t responses, PIN attempts, blocked-card state and latency. One caution: Avail=
able data supports channel behaviour but not every app screen, click or aband=
oned session.

Regards
